diff --git a/docs/bots.md b/docs/bots.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..379e839
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bots.md
@@ -0,0 +1,55 @@
+# Chat bots
+
+Any chat member can open **Account settings → Set up chat bots** to create bots.
+The bots page lists all bots owned by that member; administrators can manage all
+bots. Names are required and avatars are optional. Each bot's code is a credential,
+shown only on its owner's (or an administrator's) bots page.
+
+Bots join all open rooms, including rooms opened later. Add them to closed rooms
+or direct conversations using the usual room membership controls.
+
+## Posting
+
+```sh
+curl --fail-with-body -d "Deploy of v1.2.3 finished ✅" \
+  "https://chat.example.com/rooms/<room-id>/<bot-code>/messages"
+```
+
+The entire UTF-8 request body is literal message text, not JSON or named form
+fields. HTML is escaped, and line breaks are preserved. For files or multiline
+input, use `--data-binary @filename` or `--data-binary @-` to avoid curl stripping
+line breaks. No browser session or CSRF token is needed.
+
+Responses:
+
+- `201 Created`, with `Location: /messages/:message_id`
+- `422 Unprocessable Content` for empty/whitespace-only or invalid UTF-8 text
+- `404 Not Found` for an unknown room or one the bot cannot access
+- `401 Unauthorized` for an invalid, deleted, or banned bot's code
+
+Messages use the ordinary message callbacks, broadcasts, unread tracking, and
+push notification jobs, with the bot as the author.
+
+## Implementation and credential safety
+
+Bots are users with the `bot` role and a creator. This reuses avatars and room
+memberships without a separate author type. Bot codes contain 32 random base58
+characters and have a unique database index. Bot management only accepts name
+and avatar changes, never a role, owner, or caller-supplied code.
+
+Deleting a bot deactivates it, removes its shared-room memberships, and immediately
+rejects its code. Its old messages and author identity are retained, just as for
+a deactivated person. Human login and sign-in transfer endpoints exclude bots.
+
+The bots page is served with `Cache-Control: no-store` and is excluded from Turbo's
+page cache. Rails filters bot codes in parameters, model inspection, SQL binds,
+and request paths. The bot endpoint deliberately skips form/JSON parsing so curl's
+default form content type neither changes the message nor logs it as parameters.
+CSRF protection is skipped only on this code-authenticated endpoint.
+
+Treat bot codes like passwords: keep them out of source control and CI output,
+store them in CI secrets, and use HTTPS. Database backups contain bot credentials
+and need the same protection as other account data. Configure reverse proxies,
+access logs, and external tracing to redact `/rooms/<room-id>/<bot-code>/messages`
+URLs or omit these requests; those systems see the URL before Rails can filter it.
+If a code is exposed, delete the bot and create a replacement.
diff --git a/lib/rails_ext/bot_message_request.rb b/lib/rails_ext/bot_message_request.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..59bf718
--- /dev/null
+++ b/lib/rails_ext/bot_message_request.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,26 @@
+# Bot messages are raw text even when curl uses its default form content type.
+# Do not parse (or log) their bodies as form fields or JSON. Codes in URL path
+# segments also need explicit redaction: Rails normally only filters the query.
+module BotMessageRequest
+  PATH = %r{\A(/rooms/[^/]+/)[^/]+(/messages/?)(?=\?|\z)}
+
+  def request_parameters
+    if post? && path.match?(PATH)
+      {}
+    else
+      super
+    end
+  end
+
+  def filtered_path
+    super.sub(PATH, '\1[FILTERED]\2')
+  end
+
+  def filtered_env
+    super.transform_values do |value|
+      value.is_a?(String) ? value.sub(PATH, '\1[FILTERED]\2') : value
+    end
+  end
+end
+
+ActionDispatch::Request.prepend BotMessageRequest
